codex
Implemented a native Track B switching-set generator based on Definition 1, Theorem 2, and the Bush-type Menon designs in Section 3.2 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2108.02529v2).

All local gates pass:

- G1: 28/28 witnesses and 12/12 full 2-design checks.
- G4: 0/200,000 structured guesses.
- G6: six attacks, all 0/8.
- Reference algorithm: 391,680 operations, 8/8 solved.
- G9(c): 72 characters, 16 elements, 211 operations.

Deliverables:

- [gen_2108_02529.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/gen_2108_02529.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.02529/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The result is not yet release-ready: Terra correctly solved the exposed `easy` rung, but OpenRouter then returned HTTP 403 “key total limit exceeded” on every remaining provider call. Consequently, the shipping `hard` rung and G9 arms have no valid multi-vendor verdict. The transcripts preserve this infrastructure failure honestly; the harness must be rerun after the key limit is restored.
